    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most failed concrete repairs use a good product on the wrong damage. Rigid epoxy goes into a crack that moves every winter, a thin crack sealer gets pushed into a spall it cannot fill, or a vinyl patcher is feathered to nothing at the edge of a broken step. Concrete repair products fall into a handful of families, and each one is built for a specific kind of damage. Match the family to the damage first, then worry about brands.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="read-damage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Start With the Damage, Not the Shelf
          </h2>

          <p>
            Concrete fails in a limited number of ways, and each failure has a product family designed for it. A crack is a line of separation through the concrete. A spall is a piece of the surface that has broken away, leaving a shallow crater. A popout is a small cone-shaped pit where an aggregate particle near the surface expanded and blew the top off. Scaling is a thin layer of the surface flaking away over a wide area. Broken edges, holes and leaks are their own categories.
          </p>

          <p>
            For cracks, the two questions that matter are width and movement. Measure width with a coin or a feeler gauge: under about 1/16 inch is a hairline, 1/16 to 1/4 inch is a typical working crack, and anything over 1/4 inch usually needs backer rod or a patching material rather than sealant alone. Movement is harder to see, so mark the crack ends and check them over a season. Cracks that open in winter and close in summer are active, and they need flexible products.
          </p>

          <p>
            For surface damage, depth decides the product. Damage under about 1/8 inch can often be handled by a resurfacer applied over the whole area. Damage from 1/8 inch to an inch or two needs a patching mortar that can be built up. Anything deeper, or anything where reinforcing steel is visible, needs a structural repair mortar or a professional. Our guide to <a href="/concrete-popping-out/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete popping out</a> explains how to tell popouts, spalls and scaling apart by cause.
          </p>

          <p>
            Location matters too. Vertical surfaces need non-sag products that stay put. Horizontal slabs can take self-leveling products that flow flat. Wet basements need materials that set or react in water. Driveways and garage floors need products that tolerate tire traffic and road salt. A product that is excellent on a basement wall can be useless on a driveway, so the label phrase for concrete is not enough on its own.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Rigid products for damage that has stopped moving. Flexible products for damage that has not.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Epoxy and cement-based patches bond hard and do not stretch. Polyurethane sealants and foams flex. Put a rigid repair across a moving crack and it fails beside itself within a season.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="cracks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Hairline and Moving Cracks
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hairline cracks in slabs are mostly cosmetic. Concrete shrinks as it cures, and fine cracks in a driveway, patio or garage floor are normal. The reason to treat them is water: in freeze-thaw climates, water that gets into a crack expands when it freezes and slowly widens it. A thin liquid crack sealer, often an acrylic or latex product in a squeeze bottle, wicks into hairlines and blocks that water. It is cheap and quick, and that is all a hairline usually needs.
          </p>

          <p>
            For hairlines across a large area, a penetrating silane or siloxane sealer applied over the whole slab often does more good than chasing each crack individually. It reduces water absorption across the surface and in the fine cracks at the same time. Our guide to the <a href="/best-penetrating-concrete-sealer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best penetrating concrete sealer</a> covers the types. Combine the two approaches on a driveway with a few wider cracks: seal those individually, then seal the whole slab.
          </p>

          <p>
            Moving cracks from about 1/8 inch up to an inch or more need an elastomeric sealant, and polyurethane is the standard choice. It bonds to concrete, stretches as the slab moves and tolerates UV and foot traffic far better than acrylic fillers. Self-leveling polyurethane sealants, such as Sikaflex Self-Leveling Sealant and Quikrete Advanced Polymer Self-Leveling Sealant, flow into horizontal cracks and level themselves. Non-sag versions are tooled by hand and work on vertical faces and sloped slabs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Backer rod is not optional on wider moving cracks. A closed-cell foam rod pushed into the crack sets the sealant depth to about half the crack width and prevents the sealant from bonding to the bottom of the crack. That three-sided bond is the most common reason sealant tears. Our page on <a href="/self-leveling-concrete-crack-filler/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">self-leveling concrete crack filler</a> covers depth limits, slope problems and temperatures in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            One product family to avoid in moving cracks is the cement-based crack filler, sold in tubs or as a vinyl patcher. It fills the gap neatly, but it cures rigid and the crack reopens along one side within a winter. The same goes for epoxy in a slab crack that moves. Rigid crack fillers belong in dormant cracks, and even then a flexible sealant is often the more forgiving choice on an outdoor slab.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="structural" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Structural Cracks and Epoxy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Structural cracks are the one place where rigid epoxy is clearly the right product, but also the place where a DIY product is most often the wrong answer. Epoxy injection resins are low-viscosity two-part systems that flow into a crack and cure stronger than the surrounding concrete. Sika sells its Sikadur line of structural epoxies for exactly this kind of work. Properly injected, epoxy restores the crack to near monolithic strength.
          </p>

          <p>
            The catch is that epoxy only works if the crack has stopped moving and the cause has been dealt with. A foundation crack caused by soil pressure, settlement or frost heave will reopen beside an epoxy repair if the load is still there. That is why any horizontal crack in a basement wall, any bowing, any crack over about 1/4 inch with offset faces, or any stair-step pattern in block should be assessed before anything is injected. Our guide to <a href="/foundation-crack-repair-cost/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">foundation crack repair cost</a> outlines what that assessment usually costs.
          </p>

          <p>
            For homeowners, structural-grade epoxy usually comes in two formats. Injection kits for poured walls include surface ports, a paste and a cartridge of resin. Cartridge epoxy crack fillers for slabs, such as PC Products PC-Concrete, are thicker gels dispensed through a mixing nozzle into a cleaned crack. Both need dry concrete and temperatures within the label range, and both cure rigid. Our <a href="/concrete-repair-kit/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete repair kit</a> guide walks through the injection process step by step.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the crack leaks rather than needing strength, polyurethane injection is the better product. It reacts with water, foams and expands into the crack, sealing it through the wall thickness while staying flexible. It adds essentially no strength. Many professionals use polyurethane for the typical leaking shrinkage crack in a poured basement wall and reserve epoxy for cracks where structural rebonding actually matters.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="surface" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Spalls, Popouts and Scaled Surfaces
          </h2>

          <p>
            Concrete patching products for surface damage fall into three groups: thin resurfacers, polymer-modified patching mortars and fast-setting repair cements. Each covers a different depth range. Picking by depth is the single most useful rule here, because almost every patching failure owners report is a product applied thinner or thicker than its label allows. Feathered edges crumble, and overly thick layers of a thin-set product crack and debond.
          </p>

          <p>
            Scaled surfaces, where the top layer has flaked off across a wide area, usually from deicers or freeze-thaw, call for a resurfacer. Products like Quikrete Re-Cap and Sakrete Top 'N Bond are polymer-modified cements designed to bond in thin layers, roughly 1/16 to 1/2 inch depending on the product. They restore a uniform wearing surface over a sound slab. Our guide to the <a href="/best-concrete-resurfacer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best concrete resurfacers</a> compares them side by side.
          </p>

          <p>
            Isolated spalls from about 1/4 inch to an inch or two deep need a patching mortar that can be built up. Vinyl or polymer-modified concrete patchers, such as Quikrete Vinyl Concrete Patcher, are designed for this range. Cut the edges of the spall back to sound concrete with roughly vertical shoulders, clean out dust, dampen the surface if the label calls for it and press the patch in firmly. A thin feathered edge is where patches fail first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Popouts are small, often 1 to 2 inches across and under an inch deep, and when there are only a few, each can be filled with the same patching mortar. If popouts are scattered across a slab, the cause is usually absorptive aggregate near the surface, and a resurfacer over the whole slab gives a more uniform result than dozens of individual patches. Either way, a penetrating sealer afterward reduces the water uptake that drives further popouts.
          </p>

          <p>
            If spalling exposes rusted reinforcing steel, the problem is deeper. Corroding steel expands and pushes the concrete off it, and patching over rust just restarts the process. That repair involves cutting back behind the steel, cleaning or treating it, and using a structural repair mortar. For a driveway or patio that is spalling from winter salt, our guide on <a href="/how-to-prevent-concrete-spalling-in-winter/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">preventing concrete spalling in winter</a> covers the deicer side of the problem.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="edges" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Chipped Edges, Steps and Corners
          </h2>

          <p>
            Broken step noses and slab corners are the hardest repairs to make last, because they take impact, have little surface to bond to and are often exposed on two or three sides. Thin patchers rarely survive here. The products that hold up are fast-setting repair cements that can be built up in a form, and two-part epoxy mortars for small, high-stress pieces.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a step nose or a corner larger than a couple of inches, a form makes all the difference. Screw or clamp a board to the face of the step so its top edge sits at the finished height, chisel or saw the broken area back to sound concrete, and pack a fast-setting cement or a polymer-modified repair mortar into the space. Products like Rapid Set Cement All and Quikrete Quick-Setting Cement are commonly used for this because they set quickly and can be shaped.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some pros drive a couple of short masonry screws or stainless pins into the sound concrete before patching a large corner, leaving the heads standing proud so the patch locks around them. It is a cheap step that significantly reduces the odds of the whole patch popping off in one piece after a frost. It matters most on exterior steps where shovels and freeze-thaw both work on the repair every winter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two-part epoxy patch putties are best for small chips where the repair is only a few cubic inches. They bond very strongly, set quickly and can be carved to shape. On larger exterior repairs, epoxy is not the better choice it seems, because its thermal movement differs from concrete and big patches can debond at the edges in direct sun. They also cost far more per volume than cement patches.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="holes-leaks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Holes, Anchors and Leaking Cracks
          </h2>

          <p>
            Setting railings, posts and bolts into concrete calls for anchoring cement or anchoring epoxy rather than a general patch. Anchoring cements such as Quikrete Anchoring Cement are pourable, fast-setting and expand slightly as they cure, which locks the anchor into the hole. Anchoring epoxies dispensed from a cartridge are used for threaded rod and rebar where load ratings matter. Either beats packing a post with ordinary patching mortar, which shrinks and loosens.
          </p>

          <p>
            Holes left after removing old fasteners, pipes or anchors are handled by depth. Small, shallow holes take a vinyl patcher or an epoxy putty. Larger holes, several inches deep, are better filled with a fast-setting cement or a concrete mix in lifts. Our guide to the <a href="/best-concrete-mix/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best concrete mix</a> covers bagged options for deeper fills.
          </p>

          <p>
            Actively leaking cracks and holes in basement walls and floors are the job for hydraulic cement. It sets in a few minutes, even in contact with running water, and expands slightly as it cures so it wedges itself into the opening. The standard technique is to chisel the crack into a reverse V or dovetail so the plug locks in, then press the cement in and hold it until it sets. Our guides on the <a href="/best-hydraulic-cement/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best hydraulic cement</a> and <a href="/how-to-use-hydraulic-cement/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how to use hydraulic cement</a> go into detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hydraulic cement is a plug, not a cure. It is rigid, so a crack that keeps moving will reopen beside it, and it only seals the face of the wall. For a leaking poured wall crack, polyurethane injection seals the full depth and tends to last longer. Hydraulic cement is best for stopping a gusher today, plugging tie holes, sealing around pipe penetrations, and leaks at the joint where floor meets wall, alongside drainage fixes that relieve the water pressure behind them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="decision" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. The Decision Table for Concrete Repair Products
          </h2>

          <p>
            Here is the whole guide in one table. Find the damage in the left column, check the width or depth, and pick the product family in the middle. The right-hand column is the mistake that most often turns a good product into a failed repair. Brands within a family vary in working time, cure time and price, but the family is what decides whether the repair lasts.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Damage</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product family</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Watch out for</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hairline slab cracks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Liquid crack sealer or penetrating sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Expecting it to fill anything over 1/8 in</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Moving slab cracks 1/8 in and up</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Polyurethane sealant plus backer rod</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Three-sided bond without backer rod</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dormant structural crack</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Epoxy injection or cartridge epoxy</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Using it before the cause is fixed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Leaking poured wall crack</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Polyurethane injection</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Using it on block walls</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Active water leak or tie hole</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hydraulic cement</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Treating it as a permanent fix</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Scaled or pitted slab</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Resurfacer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Skipping pressure wash and SSD prep</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Spalls 1/4 to 2 in deep</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Polymer-modified patcher</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Feathered edges</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Broken step nose or corner</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fast-setting repair cement in a form</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No form, no mechanical key</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Railings, posts, bolts</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Anchoring cement or anchoring epoxy</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Plain patch mortar that shrinks</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Every one of these products shares the same preparation. Remove loose and weak concrete back to a sound surface. Clean out dust, oil and old sealant, because nothing bonds to dust. Pay attention to moisture: epoxy and most sealants need dry concrete, while cement patches and resurfacers usually want the surface damp with no standing water. And respect the temperature range on the label, which for most products starts around 40 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit.
          </p>

          <p>
            After the repair, protect it. A penetrating sealer over the whole slab reduces water uptake into both old and new concrete. Avoid rock salt and ammonium-based deicers for the first winter on fresh patches, and ideally for good. Our guides to <a href="/does-salt-damage-concrete/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">whether salt damages concrete</a> and the <a href="/best-ice-melt-for-concrete/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best ice melt for concrete</a> cover the safer alternatives.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are dealing with a patio specifically, our guide to the <a href="/cracked-concrete-patio/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">cracked concrete patio</a> lays out when to repair, resurface, cover or replace. For crack-specific product picks, our pillar on the <a href="/best-concrete-crack-filler/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best concrete crack filler</a> ranks individual products by the crack each one suits.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Feathering a patch to nothing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Thin edges are where patches fail first. Cement-based patchers need a minimum depth, often 1/4 inch or more, and feathered edges dry out, crack and lift. Cut the edges of the damaged area back to sound concrete with roughly vertical shoulders so the patch has a full-thickness edge everywhere it meets the old slab.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using rigid filler in a moving crack</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cement fillers, vinyl patchers and epoxy all cure rigid. In a crack that opens and closes with the seasons, the bond breaks along one side within a winter and the crack reappears next to the repair. Use a polyurethane sealant with backer rod for any crack that is still moving or reopens each year.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Patching over a contaminated surface</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dust, oil, curing compound and old sealer all prevent bonding. Owners commonly report patches that pop off in one piece, with a clean underside, which means the bond never formed. Wire brush, vacuum and degrease before patching, and pressure wash a slab before resurfacing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating hydraulic cement as permanent</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hydraulic cement stops water fast, but it is a rigid face plug. If the crack moves or water pressure stays high, it often leaks again beside the plug. Use it to stop an active leak, then deal with drainage or inject the crack properly if the leak returns.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring temperature limits</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most concrete repair products specify minimum temperatures around 40 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit during application and cure. Below that, epoxy can stay soft for days, cement patches gain strength slowly, and sealants skin over without bonding. Repairs made in late fall frequently fail first, so wait for a warm spell.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best all-purpose concrete repair product?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                There is no true all-purpose product. If you only buy one, a polymer-modified concrete patcher covers the most common surface damage, and a polyurethane sealant with backer rod covers most slab cracks. Those two handle the majority of household repairs. Structural cracks, active leaks and large areas each need their own specific product family.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use hydraulic cement on a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is rarely the right choice. Hydraulic cement is designed to set fast in wet conditions and stop leaks, not to resist traffic, deicers and freeze-thaw on an exposed slab. For driveway spalls use a polymer-modified patcher or resurfacer, and for driveway cracks use a polyurethane sealant. Save hydraulic cement for basement leaks and below-grade work.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use epoxy or polyurethane for concrete cracks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use epoxy when the crack has stopped moving and you need to rebond it for strength, typically indoors and on structural elements after the cause is fixed. Use polyurethane when the crack still moves or leaks, since it stays flexible and seals water. On outdoor slabs, polyurethane sealant is usually the safer and longer-lasting choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How deep can concrete patching products go?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the product. Resurfacers are typically rated for about 1/16 to 1/2 inch. Vinyl and polymer-modified patchers usually cover roughly 1/4 inch to an inch or two per layer. Fast-setting repair cements and concrete mixes go deeper, often in lifts. Always check the label minimum and maximum, because both too thin and too thick cause failures.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do concrete repair products match the color of old concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rarely exactly. New cement-based patches usually start darker and lighten over weeks, while old concrete has weathered. Owners commonly report patches that remain visible for a year or more. Resurfacing a whole slab gives a uniform color, and concrete stains can blend patches if appearance matters, but plan on some visible difference.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long before I can drive on a concrete repair?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It varies by product. Polyurethane sealants often allow foot traffic within a day and vehicles in a few days. Fast-setting repair cements may take vehicles within hours to a day. Resurfacers typically need around 24 hours for foot traffic and longer for vehicles. Follow the specific label, and allow more time in cool, damp weather.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When are concrete repair products not enough?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                When the concrete is moving as a whole, broken into pieces, heaving, sinking more than an inch or so, or showing structural signs like horizontal wall cracks or bowing. Repair products treat surface damage and cracks in otherwise sound concrete. Slabs that have failed structurally need leveling, partial replacement or a contractor's assessment.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

